          question: "How often should a closed-loop chilled water system be topped up with corrosion inhibitor?",
          answer: "Closed-loop inhibitor concentration should be tested every three months and topped up whenever the product concentration falls below the minimum effective level, typically 1,000\u20132,000 ppm for most phosphonate-based formulations. Concentration drops occur through system leaks, component replacements, and makeup water additions \u2014 all of which dilute the inhibitor charge. In Oman's district cooling and commercial HVAC sector, systems are often expanded or modified during summer shutdowns, introducing large volumes of untreated makeup water that require immediate inhibitor correction. Global Chemicals LLC recommends establishing a baseline chemical log and using test strips or photometric kits for quick on-site monitoring. An annual drain-and-refill is rarely justified unless microbiological contamination is found; instead, continuous top-up maintenance keeps costs low and ensures uninterrupted corrosion protection."
