      faqs: [
        {
          question: "What causes scale in boilers and how do hardness control chemicals prevent it?",
          answer: "Scale forms in boilers when dissolved calcium and magnesium salts in feed water exceed their solubility limits at elevated temperatures and precipitate as calcium carbonate, calcium sulphate, and magnesium silicate on heat transfer surfaces. A 1 mm scale layer increases fuel consumption by 5\u20138% and creates hot spots that can cause tube overheating and failure. In Oman, boiler feed water frequently comes from desalinated sources with residual hardness or from groundwater with very high natural hardness. Hardness control chemicals work through two mechanisms: chelation (EDTA-based compounds bind calcium and magnesium in soluble complexes that remain mobile and are removed by blowdown) and threshold inhibition (phosphonate polymers prevent crystal lattice growth at very low concentrations). Global Chemicals LLC supplies both all-organic and phosphate/polymer blends to match each customer's water analysis, boiler pressure, and discharge consent requirements."
        },
        {
          question: "How much blowdown is needed for an industrial boiler in Oman?",
          answer: "Blowdown rate depends on the boiler's cycles of concentration target, feed water quality, and steam purity requirements. For a typical industrial firetube boiler in Oman operating at 7\u201310 bar, a cycles of concentration of 8\u201312 is common, meaning the dissolved solids in boiler water are 8\u201312 times higher than in feed water. At this concentration, conductivity in the boiler drum typically runs 2,000\u20134,000 \xB5S/cm. Continuous blowdown of 5\u201310% of evaporation rate is used for conductivity control, supplemented by intermittent bottom blowdown to remove settled sludge. Excessive blowdown wastes expensive treated feed water and heat; insufficient blowdown allows scale-forming salts and silica to exceed safe limits. Global Chemicals LLC provides boiler water analysis and blowdown optimisation as part of its chemical treatment service, helping customers balance water savings with equipment protection."
        }
      ],
