          question: "What is MEG (monoethylene glycol) used for in oil and gas operations in Oman?",
          answer: "MEG (monoethylene glycol, ethylene glycol) is a primary hydrate inhibitor and gas dehydration chemical used extensively in Oman's oil and gas sector by PDO, OQ, and BP Oman. In subsea and deepwater gas pipelines, hydrates \u2014 ice-like crystalline structures of water and gas molecules \u2014 can form at high pressure and low temperature, blocking flow lines and creating serious safety hazards. MEG is injected at the wellhead and into pipeline risers to depress the hydrate formation temperature (thermodynamic inhibition), keeping the gas stream in the hydrate-free zone throughout transport. MEG is also used as a dehydration solvent in glycol contactors to remove water vapour from natural gas before export. In Oman's onshore gas fields, MEG injection is standard practice in wet gas gathering systems, condensate lines, and LNG feed gas conditioning. Global Chemicals LLC supplies technical-grade and recovered MEG in bulk and IBC quantities for Oman's oilfield operations."
